{
  "text_input": "Patient is a 43-year-old male who presented with a one-day history of headache in the right temporal region and sudden inability to speak (transcortical motor aphasia), associated with deviation of the angle of the mouth to the left and right-sided UMN facial palsy. He has a history of smoking (15-20 years) and alcohol consumption. MRI brain confirmed an acute infarct in the right fronto-parietal, posterior parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes. Laboratory investigations revealed polycythemia (Hb 16.7 g/dL) and elevated serum homocysteine (19.5). RFT and LFT were within normal limits (Urea 13 mg/dL, Creatinine 0.7 mg/dL). 2D Echo showed normal LV systolic function (EF 62%). He was treated with antiplatelets (Ecospirin, Clopilet), statins (Atorva), and thiamine supplementation. Specialist consultations from Neurology and Psychiatry were obtained.",
